---
/**
 * Cookie consent banner.
 *
 * Renders only when analytics is actually configured — a consent banner on a
 * site that sets no analytics cookie is a lie in the other direction, and it
 * would train visitors to dismiss a control that does nothing.
 *
 * Three decisions worth defending, because each had a plausible alternative:
 *
 * 1. **Google Consent Mode v2, not a hard gate on the script.** The strictest
 *    option is to load nothing from Google until someone clicks Accept. It is
 *    simpler, and it was tempting on a site whose whole position is that it
 *    does not fake things. It was rejected because most people never touch a
 *    banner either way, so a hard gate would have taken the site straight back
 *    to the state the audit just fixed: no idea how many people arrive or where
 *    they go. Consent Mode denies `analytics_storage` by default — no cookie is
 *    written, nothing identifies a person — while still allowing cookieless
 *    pings, so aggregate page counts survive a refusal. That is the honest
 *    trade: measure the crowd by default, never the individual, and only
 *    identify a returning browser if asked.
 *
 * 2. **Accept and Decline have equal visual weight.** Not a styled Accept
 *    beside a grey text link. Under GDPR and the EU cookie rules refusing must
 *    be as easy as agreeing, and beyond the law, a nudge here would be the one
 *    dark pattern on a site that tells people astrology cannot predict their
 *    future. Both buttons are the same size, the same shape, the same distance
 *    from the thumb.
 *
 * 3. **It is a region, not a modal.** No focus trap and no backdrop. The
 *    banner asks for a preference; it does not hold the page hostage until it
 *    gets one. It is placed early in the DOM (right after the skip link) so a
 *    keyboard user reaches it in the first few tabs rather than after 129 pages
 *    worth of navigation, while `position: fixed` keeps it visually at the
 *    bottom and — critically — out of layout flow, so it cannot shift the page.
 *    This site measures CLS 0 and it stays that way.
 *
 * Server-rendered with `hidden` and revealed by script, rather than injected.
 * Injecting markup after load is what makes banners jump. `hidden` works here
 * because `[hidden] { display: none !important }` near the top of global.css
 * makes it immune to whatever `display` a component sets — see the note in
 * docs/session-handoff.md about the twelve filters that silently ignored it.
 */
import { site } from '../config/site';
const enabled = Boolean(site.integrations.ga4Id);
---

{
  enabled && (
    <div
      class="consent"
      id="consent-banner"
      role="region"
      aria-labelledby="consent-title"
      data-consent
      hidden
    >
      <div class="consent__inner">
        {/*
          Two registers of the same disclosure: a one-line summary that is the
          whole banner on a phone, and the full explanation which shows from
          860px up.

          Measured before this change, at 375x812: the banner was 398px tall —
          49% of the viewport — and `document.elementFromPoint()` on both
          homepage hero buttons returned the banner. The site's two primary
          first-actions were physically unclickable on first visit, on the
          most-viewed page, on the viewport most people arrive on.

          The arithmetic is why this is a copy change and not only a CSS one.
          On 375x667 the hero buttons sit 159px from the bottom of the screen,
          so a bottom-docked banner has 159px to work in. A title, a paragraph,
          a disclosure control and two 48px buttons do not fit in 159px at any
          padding — collapsing the detail block got it to 224px and the buttons
          were still covered. One line and two buttons fit in ~138px.

          Nothing that makes the trade honest was dropped. "Saying no is
          completely fine" and a link to the privacy policy are both in the one
          line that always shows, and /privacy/ carries the full detail — which
          is where it properly lives. The heading stays in the DOM for
          `aria-labelledby`, visually hidden on phones only.
        */}
        <div class="consent__copy">
          <h2 class="consent__title" id="consent-title" tabindex="-1">
            A word about cookies
          </h2>
          <p class="consent__lead">
            Analytics only, nothing that identifies you. Saying no is completely fine.{' '}
            <a href="/privacy/">Privacy</a>.
          </p>
          <div class="consent__more">
            <h3 class="visually-hidden">What this means</h3>
            <p>
              I use Google Analytics to see which pages people actually read: how many visits,
              which articles, nothing that identifies you. Saying yes lets it set one cookie so a
              return visit is not counted as a new person. Saying no is completely fine: the site
              works identically, and I still get an anonymous page count.
            </p>
            <p class="consent__fine">
              Never advertising, never sold, never linked to your birth details. The{' '}
              <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> spells it out.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="consent__actions">
          <button class="btn btn--accent" type="button" data-consent-accept>
            Yes, that's fine
          </button>
          <button class="btn btn--ghost" type="button" data-consent-decline>
            No thanks
          </button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  )
}

{/*
  `set:html` rather than putting the code between the script tags.
  `<script is:inline>{\`…\`}</script>` inside a component emits the braces and
  backticks *literally*, so the shipped page contained `<script>{\`(function
  () {…})();\`}</script>` — a block statement wrapping an unused template
  string. Perfectly valid JavaScript that does nothing at all, with no error
  and no console warning: the banner simply never appeared. This is the same
  family of bug as the `hidden` attribute note in the handoff — the failure
  mode here is silence, so verify the behaviour in a browser, never the
  presence of the markup. BaseLayout.astro already used `set:html` for the
  gtag block; this now matches it.
*/}
{
  enabled && (
    <script
      is:inline
      set:html={`
      (function () {
        var KEY = 'ml-consent-v1';
        var banner = document.querySelector('[data-consent]');
        if (!banner) return;

        function read() {
          try { return localStorage.getItem(KEY); } catch (e) { return null; }
        }
        function write(v) {
          try { localStorage.setItem(KEY, v); } catch (e) { /* private mode: choice lasts this page only */ }
        }
        function apply(v) {
          if (typeof window.gtag === 'function') {
            window.gtag('consent', 'update', {
              analytics_storage: v === 'granted' ? 'granted' : 'denied'
            });
          }
        }
        function close(v) {
          write(v);
          apply(v);
          banner.hidden = true;
          // Returning focus matters when the banner was opened deliberately
          // from the footer; on first visit the opener does not exist and
          // focus simply stays where the visitor left it.
          var opener = document.querySelector('[data-consent-reopen]');
          if (opener && banner.dataset.reopened === 'true') opener.focus();
          banner.dataset.reopened = 'false';
        }

        banner.querySelector('[data-consent-accept]').addEventListener('click', function () { close('granted'); });
        banner.querySelector('[data-consent-decline]').addEventListener('click', function () { close('denied'); });

        // Only ask people who have not answered. A stored 'denied' is an
        // answer, and re-asking someone who said no is the behaviour that
        // makes these banners hated.
        if (!read()) banner.hidden = false;

        // Let anyone change their mind, from every page.
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-consent-reopen]').forEach(function (btn) {
          btn.addEventListener('click', function () {
            banner.dataset.reopened = 'true';
            banner.hidden = false;
            banner.querySelector('.consent__title').focus();
          });
        });
      })();
      `}
    />
  )
}
